Windows-only LLVM fix. scripts/build_llvm.ps1 applies it after the series in
thirdparty/llvm-build-info.json; build_llvm.sh and the CI MLIR cache key never
read thirdparty/llvm-windows/, so Linux builds are unchanged.

MLIR's ExecutionEngine reserves one contiguous block per JIT object
(SectionMemoryManager's reserveAllocationSpace) only on AArch64. On COFF x86-64,
RuntimeDyld aborts with "IMAGE_REL_AMD64_ADDR32NB relocation requires an ordered
section layout" whenever an object's sections land more than 4GB apart, which
happens at random once a process has loaded many kernels (llvm/llvm-project#55386,
#65641).

Upstream status: submitted as https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/pull/233658.

diff --git a/mlir/lib/ExecutionEngine/ExecutionEngine.cpp b/mlir/lib/ExecutionEngine/ExecutionEngine.cpp
index 49a88e000..25b84ba12 100644
--- a/mlir/lib/ExecutionEngine/ExecutionEngine.cpp
+++ b/mlir/lib/ExecutionEngine/ExecutionEngine.cpp
@@ -315,8 +315,10 @@ ExecutionEngine::create(Operation *m, const ExecutionEngineOptions &options,
                                        llvm::jitlink::JITLinkMemoryManager
                                            &IgnoredMemMgr) {
     // Needed to respect AArch64 ABI requirements on the distance between
-    // TEXT and GOT sections.
-    bool reserveAlloc = llvmModule->getTargetTriple().isAArch64();
+    // TEXT and GOT sections, and for COFF's image-relative (ADDR32NB)
+    // relocations in .pdata, which need code < rodata < rwdata within 4GB.
+    const llvm::Triple &hostTriple = llvmModule->getTargetTriple();
+    bool reserveAlloc = hostTriple.isAArch64() || hostTriple.isOSBinFormatCOFF();
     auto objectLayer = std::make_unique<RTDyldObjectLinkingLayer>(
         session, [sectionMemoryMapper = options.sectionMemoryMapper,
                   reserveAlloc](const MemoryBuffer &) {
